<!--
  Tiered storage: local NVMe for the hot window, object storage for everything older
  (project.md §3.4, §33, §35, Appendix A).

  This is the answer to the question a bounded VPS disk always raises. §4.2 puts a billion
  events a day at roughly a terabyte before compression, so no local disk is a retention
  plan — and §33's "3-14 day hot window" reads like a retention ceiling when it is really a
  statement about which disk the bytes are on.

  A storage policy separates the two. `events_raw` names the policy, not a disk, so the
  schema is identical everywhere and the deployment decides what "cold" means:

      development   a second local directory, so the mechanism is exercised for real
      production    a GCS bucket, where the volume is effectively unbounded and billed
                    per gigabyte

  That is exactly how managed ClickHouse appears to have unlimited storage. It is not a
  larger disk; it is object storage as the primary store with local disk as a cache. The
  same arrangement is available here, which is why §35's "Stage 2 hot/cold simplification"
  does not have to wait for Stage 2.

  The archive (§14) stays where it is and is not replaced by this. It is Parquet, it is
  portable, and it is the copy that survives losing the cluster — the cold volume is a
  ClickHouse-internal format that only ClickHouse can read.

  ## The disk under `cold` in production

  GCS through its S3-compatible XML API, which is what ClickHouse speaks:

      <cold>
          <type>s3</type>
          <endpoint>https://storage.googleapis.com/BUCKET/clickhouse/</endpoint>
          <access_key_id from_env="GCS_HMAC_ACCESS_KEY"/>
          <secret_access_key from_env="GCS_HMAC_SECRET"/>
          <support_batch_delete>false</support_batch_delete>
      </cold>

  The credentials are read from the environment rather than written here, because this file
  is in version control and an HMAC key in version control is a leaked HMAC key.
  `support_batch_delete` is false because GCS does not implement S3's batch delete.

  A `<cache>` disk sits in front of it, so a granule read once is served from local NVMe the
  next time. That is the other half of how managed deployments make object storage feel
  local.

  ## Why the object disks are not in this file

  They used to be, guarded by `from_env`, with a comment claiming that a deployment without
  the variables set "simply has no cold_object disk". That was false. ClickHouse constructs
  every declared disk eagerly — Keeper's own disk selector does it before the server is
  ready — so an unset endpoint becomes an empty URL and the server refuses to start with
  `Code: 36 ... Empty protocol in the URL`. CI found it on the first run; it had never been
  noticed locally because the developer machine had real credentials in .env, so the
  fallback this file described had never once executed.

  So the split is by file rather than by conditional. This one is what every stack gets and
  what CI runs: a second local directory, which exercises the policy and the part movement
  for real. `clickhouse/cold-object.xml` holds the object-store variant and is mounted only
  where there is a bucket to point it at — see the commented volume line in
  docker-compose.yml. It merges after this file and replaces the cold volume's disk, so the
  schema still names the *policy* and `events_raw` never knows the difference.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <storage_configuration>
        <disks>
            <!--
              Deliberately outside /var/lib/clickhouse. A disk nested inside the default
              path makes the server's own accounting ambiguous — it would see the cold
              volume's bytes twice, once as themselves and once as part of the default
              disk — and the disk-space guard in Appendix A reads exactly that number.
            -->
            <cold_local>
                <type>local</type>
                <path>/var/lib/clickhouse-cold/</path>
            </cold_local>
        </disks>

        <policies>
            <hot_cold>
                <volumes>
                    <!--
                      Named `default`, not `hot`, and the name is load-bearing rather than
                      cosmetic. ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY SETTING storage_policy accepts a new
                      policy only if it contains the *volumes* of the old one — not merely
                      its disks — so a table already on the `default` policy can move here
                      only while a volume of that name still exists. Calling it `hot` reads
                      better and makes the migration fail with "New storage policy
                      `hot_cold` shall contain volumes of the old storage policy `default`".
                    -->
                    <default>
                        <disk>default</disk>
                    </default>
                    <cold>
                        <disk>cold_local</disk>
                        <!--
                          Merging parts that already live on the cold volume rewrites
                          them there, which on object storage is a download, a merge and
                          an upload for no query benefit — the data is past the hot window
                          and is read rarely. Merges still happen on the hot volume, where
                          §25's insert path needs them.
                        -->
                        <prefer_not_to_merge>true</prefer_not_to_merge>
                    </cold>
                </volumes>
                <!--
                  Move off the hot volume before the disk is full rather than when it is.
                  Appendix A's guard escalates at 80% and 85%; this keeps ordinary
                  operation below that, so the guard stays an alarm rather than a routine
                  event.
                -->
                <move_factor>0.2</move_factor>
            </hot_cold>
        </policies>
    </storage_configuration>
</clickhouse>
